{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Design Draft and Theory-to-Prototype Alignment","title":"README and Repository Disclosure","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting readme and repository disclosure to prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"07","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/04/06"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_artifact_signal"],"ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# README and Repository Disclosure\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting AI Use Disclosure, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/04-07-readme-and-repository-disclosure.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for README and Repository Disclosure: AI Use Disclosure, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\n\"AI was used in this project\" is a sentence that satisfies no one. It names no tool, no location, and no human edit, so a reviewer cannot check it, and an instructor cannot tell whether the work demonstrates the author's judgment or the model's defaults. A disclosure earns trust only when it is auditable: tied to the artifact, specific about who did what, and verifiable against the evidence (the commit history) that the repository already keeps. AIL 606 treats the README as the place where two obligations meet, honest disclosure of AI use and a traceability matrix linking each principle to each screen change, both of them checkable by anyone who clones the repo.\n\nThis section teaches you to write disclosures and traceability that a reviewer can verify against the commit log rather than take on faith. You should leave able to convert a vague integrity claim into evidence, and able to colocate the disclosure with the artifact so the claim and the proof live in the same place.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write AI-use disclosures that name the tool, the location, and the author's follow-up edit.\n- Tie each disclosure claim to verifiable evidence (a commit) in the repository.\n- Build a theory-to-screen traceability matrix that a reviewer can audit.\n- Colocate disclosure and artifact so integrity is evidenced, not merely asserted.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA reviewer clones two AIL 606 prototypes. The first README says \"AI was used in this project. The design follows good learning principles.\" There is nothing to check: no tool named, no screen tied to a principle, no link to the history. The second README lists \"Codex generated the flashcard component (commit a3f9c1); I rewrote the scoring to normalize case (commit b7d2e4),\" followed by a table mapping each screen to its controlling principle. The reviewer opens the diff for commit b7d2e4 and confirms the scoring change with their own eyes. One README asserts integrity; the other lets the reviewer verify it.\n\nThe case shows the gap between claiming and evidencing. The first author may be perfectly honest, but honesty that cannot be checked carries no weight in a review.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nDisclosure is the documented, auditable account of how AI tools contributed to an artifact and what the human author did with the output. Three properties make a disclosure auditable. Specificity: it names the tool, the exact location (which component, which figure), and the author's follow-up edit, so a reader knows what was generated versus authored. Linkage: each claim points to a commit, so the assertion can be checked against the diff the version-control system already records. Colocation: the disclosure lives with the artifact in the repository, not in a separate document, so the claim and its evidence cannot drift apart.\n\nTraceability in the README serves the parallel function for design: a matrix mapping each screen change to the controlling principle that justified it. This is the public, auditable form of the principle-to-feature work, written so a cloner can read down the table and then open the corresponding screen to check the mapping. Together, disclosure and traceability turn the repository into a self-verifying record: the README states the claims, the commit history and the screens supply the proof.\n\n| Vague claim | Auditable disclosure |\n| --- | --- |\n| \"AI was used\" | \"Codex generated the flashcard component (commit a3f9c1)\" |\n| \"I edited it\" | \"I rewrote the scoring to normalize case (commit b7d2e4) after testing Paris and paris\" |\n| \"Good learning principles\" | A table mapping each screen to its controlling principle |\n\nThe reason version control is central is that a commit is tamper-evident evidence with an author and a timestamp. Pointing a disclosure at a commit converts an honor-system statement into something a reviewer can independently confirm, which is the entire difference between claiming integrity and evidencing it.\n\nAn auditable disclosure is written from the commit history backward, in a definite order. Arrange the steps so specificity, linkage, and colocation all fall out of the process.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Open the commit log and find each commit where AI output entered or was revised\", \"For each, write a line naming the tool, the exact location, and what you changed\", \"End each line with the commit hash so the claim links to a verifiable diff\", \"Build the traceability table mapping each screen to its controlling principle\", \"Colocate disclosure and table in the README and clone fresh to confirm every link resolves\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A README states only that AI was used in the project and that the design follows good learning principles. Why does this satisfy no reviewer even if the author is perfectly honest?\" explanation=\"Honesty that cannot be checked carries no weight in a review. The sentence names no tool, no location, no author edit, and links to no commit, so there is nothing a cloner can verify against the evidence the repository already keeps.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It names no tool, location, or edit and links to no commit, so nothing in it can be verified.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is too short; a longer paragraph would make it auditable.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It belongs in a separate file rather than the README.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is fine; stating that AI was used is the whole obligation.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWork through one annotated before/after revision of a README's AI-use disclosure and traceability section.\n\n**Before (README excerpt):**\n\n```markdown\n## About\nThis is my AIL 606 prototype. AI was used in this project.\nThe design follows good learning principles.\n```\n\n**Annotations a reviewer would leave:**\n\n1. \"AI was used\" names no tool, no location, and no author edit, so it cannot be verified or audited.\n2. \"Good learning principles\" asserts quality without tracing any principle to any screen change.\n3. The disclosure lives only in prose with no link to the commit history, so a cloner cannot check it.\n\n**After (revised README excerpt):**\n\n```markdown\n## AI Use Disclosure\n- Codex generated the flashcard component (commit a3f9c1). I rewrote the\n  scoring to normalize case (commit b7d2e4) after testing \"Paris\" and \"paris\".\n- ChatGPT drafted alt text for Figures 1 to 3; I revised each to match the\n  diagram content (commit c1a8f0).\n\n## Theory-to-Screen Traceability\n| Screen | Principle | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Practice 1 | Signaling | Highlight synced to the prompt term |\n| Quiz | Feedback timing | Per-item feedback for formative items |\n```\n\n**Why the after version is stronger:** every AI claim names the tool, the commit, and the author's follow-up edit, so a reviewer cloning the repo can check each one against the diff. The traceability table ties each screen change to a specific principle. The disclosure and the artifact now live together and are auditable, which is the difference between claiming integrity and evidencing it.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why colocation (keeping the disclosure inside the README beside the artifact) does work that a specific, commit-linked disclosure in a separate document cannot. What failure does colocation prevent?\" answer=\"A specific, linked disclosure can still be trustworthy on its own, but if it lives in a separate file the claim and its evidence can drift apart: the README can be updated while the separate disclosure goes stale, or a reviewer who clones the repo may never see it. Colocation binds the claim to the artifact in the same place a cloner already opens, so the disclosure travels with the work and the evidence (commits, screens) sits right next to the claims it supports. It prevents the silent divergence between what the project says about its AI use and what the repository actually contains.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWrite every disclosure so a stranger with the repo could verify it. For each claim:\n\n1. **Specificity check:** Does it name the tool, the location, and the author's edit?\n2. **Linkage check:** Does it point to a commit a reviewer can open and confirm?\n3. **Colocation check:** Does the disclosure live with the artifact, and does the traceability table map each screen to its principle?\n\nIf a claim cannot be checked against the commit history, it is an assertion, not a disclosure.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first error is the catch-all sentence (\"AI was used\"), which feels like disclosure but discloses nothing checkable. The second is asserting quality (\"follows good principles\") without a traceability table that ties principles to actual screens. The third is keeping the disclosure in a separate file from the artifact, so the claim and the evidence can drift apart and neither can be trusted.\n\nDisclosure is verifiable evidence colocated with the work, not a polite acknowledgment that a tool was somewhere involved.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWrite the disclosure from the commit history backward. Open the log, find each commit where AI output entered or was revised, and write a line that names the tool, the location, and what you changed, ending with the commit hash. Sourcing the disclosure from the actual history guarantees specificity and linkage at once.\n\nThen build the traceability table as the public face of your principle-to-feature work. Each row maps a screen to the one principle that controls its key change, written so a reader can open that screen and check the claim. The table is the bridge between your internal design reasoning and an external reviewer's ability to audit it.\n\nFinally, colocate everything in the README and confirm the links resolve. A disclosure that points to a commit that does not exist, or a table that names a screen that is not in the repo, is worse than none, because it claims verifiability it cannot deliver. Clone your own repo fresh and check every link as a reviewer would.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence** on the right. The claim is a disclosure or traceability statement; the evidence is the commit or screen a reviewer would open to confirm it. By the end of AIL 606 04.07, your notes should let a stranger audit the repository without asking you anything.\n\nA strong reader of a README disclosure can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which tool did what, where, and what did the author change afterward?\n2. Which commit would a reviewer open to verify each claim?\n3. Does the traceability table map each screen to a checkable principle?\n4. Do the disclosure and the artifact live together so the evidence cannot drift?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when every AI claim is specific, linked to a commit, and colocated with the work, and when the traceability table lets a reviewer audit each principle-to-screen mapping. Treat a catch-all sentence as a non-disclosure. Where a claim lacks a commit link, mark it with the annotation tools and ask BigAL to compare a vague disclosure with an auditable one rather than for a generic statement about academic integrity.\n\nA useful final paragraph for the artifact would sound like this: \"I rewrote the disclosure to name each tool, location, and my follow-up edit with its commit hash, and added a screen-to-principle table. I rejected the suggestion to summarize all AI use in one sentence, because a reviewer could not verify a single specific claim from it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same disclosure obligation from a course prototype to a published open-source educational tool with outside contributors. The properties hold, but the stakes and audience grow: disclosure now serves not just an instructor but every downstream user and contributor, the traceability table becomes part of the project's documentation, and the commit linkage must survive across many authors. Transfer means scaling the same auditable structure to a multi-author repository, not abandoning it because the project got bigger. If you understand disclosure as evidence, a larger audience raises the bar for verifiability rather than lowering it.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/04-07-readme-and-repository-disclosure-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"04.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[the COPE position statement on authorship and AI tools](https://publicationethics.org/guidance/cope-position/authorship-and-ai-tools)**, which requires disclosing which tool was used and how.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes for AIL 606 04.07. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to the specificity-linkage-colocation standard for [auditable AI-use disclosure](https://publicationethics.org/guidance/cope-position/authorship-and-ai-tools). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_readme_and_repository_disclosure\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ai_use_disclosure\", \"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm that every disclosure names a tool, location, and edit linked to a commit, and that the traceability table maps each screen to a checkable principle, all colocated in the README. That auditability is what evidences integrity rather than asserting it.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_use_disclosure","stem":"Which README AI-use disclosure is specific enough to be defensible for a graded prototype?","options":["\"AI was used in this project.\"","\"Codex generated the initial flashcard component (commit a3f9); I rewrote the scoring to normalize case and authored all card content and feedback. ChatGPT was used to draft, then I revised, the alt text for Figures 1 to 3.\"","\"No AI was used, mostly.\"","\"AI helped a little here and there.\""],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible disclosure names what tool did what, where (commit or section), and what the author then changed. Blanket statements neither inform a reader nor support integrity review.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_disclosure_blanket_statement","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_use_disclosure"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_use_disclosure","stem":"Why does a README traceability matrix linking theory to screen changes belong in the repository rather than only in a separate report?","options":["Because READMEs must always be long.","Because a reviewer cloning the repo can then see, at the point of the code and assets, which design principle each screen change served, keeping the disclosure and the artifact together and auditable.","Because separate reports are not allowed in this course.","Because it makes the repository look more complete."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Co-locating the traceability and disclosure with the code lets any reader audit the design reasoning against the actual artifact, rather than trusting a detached document.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_disclosure_detached_from_artifact","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ai_use_disclosure"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-01","ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to write your README's AI-use disclosure for you. What is the strongest bounded use?","options":["Accept whatever it writes, since it knows the right format.","Use it for the structure and headings, but fill in the specific tools, commits, and your own edits yourself, because only you know what AI actually did in this project.","Skip the disclosure since the AI wrote part of the README anyway.","Ask the AI to also guess which commits used AI."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An AI cannot truthfully report its own role in your specific project. The author must supply the actual tools, locations, and edits; AI can only help with formatting.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_disclosure_ai_writes_its_own_record","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_readme_and_repository_disclosure_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"A reviewer says your repository \"discloses AI use but I cannot verify any of it.\" Which repository practice most directly fixes this?","options":["Add a longer paragraph claiming the work is original.","Keep a commit history where AI-generated additions and your subsequent edits appear as separate commits, so the disclosure claims can be checked against the diff.","Delete the AI-generated commits to keep the history clean.","Move the disclosure to the bottom of the README."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Verifiability comes from a commit history that lets a reviewer trace generated content and the author's revisions. Squashing or deleting that history removes the evidence the disclosure rests on.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_disclosure_unverifiable_claims","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-07-lo-02"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_disclosure_blanket_statement","pattern":"blanket_ai_disclosure","description":"Disclosing AI use with a vague blanket statement that names no tool, location, or author edit","trigger":"README says AI was used without specifying what, where, or how it was revised","feedback":"Name the tool, the location (commit or section), and what you changed afterward. A blanket statement is not a usable disclosure.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_disclosure_detached_from_artifact","pattern":"disclosure_detached_from_artifact","description":"Keeping the disclosure or traceability separate from the repository so a reader cannot check it against the code","trigger":"design reasoning lives only in an external report, not alongside the code and assets","feedback":"Co-locate the traceability matrix and disclosure in the repo so each screen change can be audited against the artifact.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_disclosure_ai_writes_its_own_record","pattern":"ai_authored_disclosure","description":"Letting an AI write the disclosure of its own role, which it cannot truthfully report","trigger":"learner accepts an AI-written account of what AI did in the project","feedback":"Use AI only for the format. Supply the actual tools, commits, and edits yourself, since only you know what AI did here.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_7_disclosure_unverifiable_claims","pattern":"unverifiable_disclosure","description":"Making disclosure claims that cannot be checked because the commit history does not separate generated content from author edits","trigger":"AI additions and author revisions are squashed or deleted from history","feedback":"Keep AI-generated additions and your edits as separate commits so the disclosure can be verified against the diff.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"ff0855e2b0e5aea99b1b1f4490f4977899564054b4dec451667ddb211865f7bf","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}